== A Servant’s Obedience to His Master

=== §. 10. #emph[Of servants’ obedience.]

No inferiors are more bound to obedience than servants: it is their main and most distinctive function to #emph[obey their masters.] It is therefore expressly mentioned here in my text #emph[(Servants, be obedient to those who are your#super[+] masters)], and all other duties are included under it. #footnote[Treatise 3, §. 17.] The reasons given to move wives and children to obey ought much more to move servants. #footnote[Treatise 5, §. 11.]

Those who are of a contrary mind, who are rebellious and disdain to be under the authority of another, and are ready to say of their master, “We do not want this man to rule over us,”#footnote[Luke 19:14.] are fitter to live among Anabaptists than among orthodox Christians. For to what end is the lawfulness of authority acknowledged, if subjection is not yielded to it? Of the two, a man would do better to be blinded by error than not to obey the truth which he knows.

In handling this point of obedience, I will follow the same order as I did in setting out the obedience of wives and children, and consider:

+ The parts of servants’ obedience, in which it consists.
+ Its extent, how far it reaches.

The parts are two: one negative, the other affirmative.

The #emph[negative] is to refrain from doing things on their own initiative, without or against their masters’ consent.

The #emph[affirmative] is readily to yield to doing what their masters would have them do.

=== §. 11. #emph[Of servants’ refraining from doing things without their masters’ consent.]

Servants ought to refrain from doing things on their own initiative without or against the consent of their masters, because while the time of their service lasts they are not their own, and the things they do ought not to be for themselves: both their persons and their actions are all their masters’, and the will of their master must be their rule and guide (in things which are not against God’s will). The rite used under the law of #emph[piercing a servant’s ear]#footnote[Exod 21:6.] implied as much. David therefore, alluding to that rite, says to God (both of himself and also of Christ, whom he prefigured as a type), “my ears You have pierced,”#footnote[Ps 40:6.] and from this he infers that he would do the will of God. We have a notable pattern of this in Abraham’s servant,#footnote[Gen 24:5.] who, in a business committed to his charge, put forward such doubts as came into his head, to receive his master’s direction about them, so that he would not be forced to do something on his own initiative without particular warrant from his master.

This general point will be made clearer if it is illustrated by some particulars. Take therefore these instances, gathered out of the Scriptures, of things which servants must not do without their masters’ consent.

1\. Servants must not go wherever they wish. The phrase the centurion uses (“I tell one to go, and he goes”)#footnote[Matt 8:9.] implies that unless his master tells him to go, he ought not to go.

2\. They ought not to do their own business and affairs. It is noted of Jacob, when he was Laban’s servant, that though he #footnote[Gen 30:35, 36.] had flocks of his own, yet he fed his master’s flocks and committed his own to his sons.

3\. They ought not to do whatever business they please. Ahimaaz had a great desire to carry the news of Absalom’s #footnote[2 Sam 18:19, #emph[etc.]] death to David; yet without the leave of Joab, his captain and master, he would not do it. The good mistress provides “portions for her maidservants,” namely, the portion of work: #footnote[Prov 31:15.] so they must do what she gives them to do. The servants of the centurion each did as their master #footnote[Matt 8:9. Matt 25:16, 17.] charged them; and the servants who had talents committed to them each looked to his own talent.

4\. They ought not to marry while the time of their contract of service lasts, unless their master gives consent to it. The law of God sets out the lawful marriages of servants in this way: “If his master gives him a wife.”#footnote[Exod 21:4.] By this it is implied that if a servant marries, it must be with his master’s consent.

#emph[Objection.] The apostle says, without excepting servants, “to avoid fornication, each man should have his own wife,” etc. #footnote[1 Cor 7:2.]

#emph[Answer.] He shows there what means the Lord has sanctified for everyone to avoid fornication; but he does not by this give everyone liberty to use that means rashly, against the order which God has set down.

2\. That precept is given to those who are #footnote[Sui juris.] #emph[in their own power.] For of children he says that #footnote[1 Cor 7:36, #emph[etc.]] parents must see what is fitting or not fitting for them.

3\. What servants are to gather from it is this: if need so requires, to make their desire known to their master, and to use all the good means they can, by themselves or others, to obtain their master’s consent.

5\. They ought not to dispose of their master’s goods at their own pleasure, not even for charitable uses. The steward who #footnote[Luke 16:1, 2.] wasted his master’s goods was justly put out of office for it. Indeed, he is called #emph[unjust] for disposing of some of them for his own #footnote[#emph[v.] 8.] future livelihood; therefore it was an act of injustice, and unlawful.

#emph[Objection.] The lord commended him for it.

#emph[Answer.] He commended his wisdom, not his injustice: his prudent care for the time to come, not the means of providing for himself. For his foresight in general he is commended; for the particular means, deceiving his master, he is called #emph[unjust.]

6\. They must not go away from their master before their contracted time has expired. When Jacob,#footnote[Gen 30:26.] after long service, had a mind to be gone, he asked leave; and because his uncle and master would not willingly let him go, he stayed on.

#emph[Objection.] Afterward Jacob#footnote[Gen 31:20.] secretly stole away from his master.

#emph[Answer.] 1. His contracted time was out. 2. He had an express warrant from God to go. 3. His manner of going away is not to be justified, and in that respect it is no good pattern.

=== §. 12. #emph[Of the unlawful liberty which servants take for themselves.]

Contrary to the limits on servants’ liberty named above are these and similar wicked and licentious tricks, as follow.

1\. When servants watch for their chance to go wherever they please without their master knowing it: as when their masters are busy with serious matters, or out of the house, or in bed. In this way that wicked servant Gehazi watched for his chance to run after Naaman, thinking that #footnote[2 Kgs 5:20.] his master would not know it.

2\. When, being bound to their master’s service, they do their own business and seek their own profit, and that without their master’s leave. What made Gehazi’s secret stealing away and withdrawing from his master worse was that he went to receive gifts for himself. There are many such wicked servants who, knowing certain friends of their master who will be ready to do them any kindness, will use their master’s name to borrow money or get some other favor, and never let their masters know of it.

3\. When servants will choose their own work and do what pleases them best, or else do nothing at all. So where many servants are in one house together, if they are not in such positions as they like, they will mutter and grumble and do nothing well.

4\. When servants, especially maidservants, who are bound, deliberately marry to free themselves; because our laws free a maid who is married from her service to master and mistress.

5\. When they are free with their master’s goods, giving them away. Some think that because they belong to the house, they may give away the things of the house to charitable uses. But the pretext of charity is no excuse for injustice. Servants may let their masters or mistresses know that there are such and such things in the house fit to be given away, or that there are such and such poor people in great need; but secretly, without any consent at all, they must not give away anything of their master’s.

6\. When, out of discontent, they run away from their masters. The two servants of Shimei who ran from their master #footnote[1 Kgs 2:39. #emph[Philem. v.] 12. Gen 16:9.] are blamed for it by the Holy Spirit. Onesimus, who ran from his master, is sent back by Saint Paul; and Hagar is sent back by an angel.

#emph[Objection.] What if master and mistress are harsh, severe and cruel?

#emph[Answer.] An angel from heaven gives one answer (“Submit #footnote[Gen 16:9.] to her authority”), and an apostle gives another: “Submit yourselves#super[+] with all fear … to the perverse. For conscience toward #footnote[1 Pet 2:18, 19.] God, endure grief, suffering wrongfully.”

These faults are noted in this way so that servants, taking notice of them, may avoid them all the more carefully.

=== §. 13. #emph[Of servants’ obedience to their masters’ commandments.]

The affirmative and active part of servants’ obedience, consisting in a ready yielding to what their masters would have done, has respect (like the same kind of obedience in children) to the #emph[commandment, instruction, reproof,] and #emph[correction] of their master.

I. Since a master has power to command his servants, it is #footnote[What masters command, servants must do.] the duty of servants to obey their master in this. The particular work which belongs to a servant by virtue of his place is to keep an eye on his master, to see what he requires of him. David describes the character of good servants in this way: “the eyes of servants look to the hand of their master.”#footnote[Ps 123:2.] That #emph[looking,] as it implies an expectation of relief and help, so it also implies a readiness to receive and carry out anything their masters would have done. The centurion commends this duty #footnote[Matt 8:9.] in the example of his servants, each of whom did what their master commanded them to do. It is further commended in the examples of Abraham’s#footnote[Gen 24:9.] servant, Elijah’s#footnote[1 Kgs 18:43.] servant, and many others. Note how far the Lord Christ demands this duty of servants: #footnote[Luke 17:7, 8.] though a servant has been plowing all day (a laborious and wearisome work), yet when he comes home and his master commands him to prepare his supper and wait on him, he must do it. By all these proofs it is clear that if a master bids his servant come, go, do this or that, he must obey.

The contrary of this is the highest degree of disobedience, #footnote[Contrary. Refusing to be at command. 2 Sam 19:26.] as when servants refuse to be at their masters’ command and to do what they charge them to do: like Ziba, who, being commanded to saddle his master’s donkey, went away and did not do it; and Job’s servants, who would not answer when they were called. #footnote[Job 19:16.] Of all offenses this most provokes masters, for it is a plain contempt of their authority.

=== §. 14. #emph[Of servants’ heeding their masters’ instructions in matters of their calling.]

#smallcaps[II]. As a master has power to command, so it is his duty to #footnote[See Treatise 8, §. 21, 38.] instruct his servants in the way of righteousness, and in the secular course of life in which he is to walk. The duty of servants, then, is to listen to them and obey them in both.

1\. Servants who are under masters to learn their trade are bound in many ways to listen to them:

1\. So that during their term they may do better service to their masters.

2\. So that by learning a trade or skill in some good calling they may be better able, when the time of their service is out, to support themselves, to teach other servants who will be under them, and to do more good in the place where they will live.

3\. So that in this way they may better keep a good conscience in the particular place in which God has set them.

Contrary to this is the idle, sluggish, dull disposition of many servants who, through their carelessness and unruliness in doing what they are taught, vex and grieve their masters, and indeed make them weary of instructing them. Many apprentices spend all the time of their apprenticeship without reaping any good at all: they never turn out masters of their craft. So many in the country live under good farmers, many clerks under good lawyers, many maids under good homemakers, and that for many years on end, yet through their negligence get no good at all; whereas if they had been attentive and careful, they might have learned much. These are enemies to their masters, to themselves, to the city and country where they live, and to their relatives and parents, especially if they have any living.

=== §. 15. #emph[Of servants’ heeding their masters’ instructions in piety.]

Servants who have religious masters, who take care to #footnote[Obedience of servants to pious instructions.] instruct their household in the way of righteousness, ought to be obedient to their instructions. So were Joshua’s servants (or else Joshua could not have answered for them #footnote[Josh 24:15.] as he did); and the servants of that official of whom it was said that “he believed, #footnote[John 4:53.] and all his house”; and the servants of Lydia and of the jailer, of whom it is said, “she and her household were baptized,”#footnote[Acts 16:15.] and “he and all his house … had come to believe in God.”#footnote[Acts 16:34.] No one doubts that #emph[servants] are included under these words #emph[house] and #emph[household.]

Now then, if the servants of these had not listened and yielded to the instructions of their master and mistress, would they have believed in him in whom their masters believed, or been baptized when their mistress was? The same may be gathered from this phrase, “the church in their house”;#footnote[Rom 16:5. Phlm 2.] for by this it is implied that all in the house were of the same faith as their master and mistress. It is expressly noted of the #footnote[Acts 10:7.] soldier who waited continually on Cornelius that he was #emph[devout,] by which it is presupposed that he listened to his master’s instructions; for it is noted of the master also that he was “devout and God-fearing.”#footnote[#emph[v.] 2.]

The benefit that comes to servants by obeying such instructions is beyond words. Much more good may be gained by following the spiritual directions of religious masters than the clever and profitable directions of the wisest statesmen and the most skillful craftsmen or tradesmen there are. So there is a double bond to bind servants to obey this kind of instruction:

+ The bond of duty.
+ The bond of profit and benefit to themselves.

=== §. 16. #emph[Of servants’ faults contrary to obedience in matters of religion.]

Though the duty named above is so needful to servants, yet of all duties it is by many least thought to be a duty, and most neglected, the very opposite being for the most part practiced, as may appear by these particulars.

1\. Many servants are of so ungodly a mind that of all masters #footnote[Contrary, 1. Unwilling to serve religious masters.] they will not serve those who are religious and make it a matter of conscience to instruct their servants in the way to salvation: they would rather serve profane masters, out-and-out worldlings, and even papists. Why? Anything agrees better with corrupt nature than to be kept within the bounds of religion. Few like the narrow gate and narrow way that leads to life; for the god of this world so blinds men’s eyes that they cannot see the blessing of life at the end of this way: they see only how narrow it is. Or if they should see it far off, yet they are so set on the things of the flesh and of this present evil world that they would not regard what is so far off. Now religious masters will do what they can to keep all their servants in that narrow way; but irreligious masters will allow them to follow the sway of their own corruption: to swear, to profane the Sabbath and spend that whole day in sleeping, play, eating and drinking; to go to bed without prayer, or catechizing, or reading the word of God, and so to rise again and go about their business. To these holy duties such devout masters as Cornelius was will hold their servants.

2\. Others, who have been placed by their relatives under such devout #footnote[2\. Mutter against them while in service.] masters, or who, not knowing the disposition of their masters beforehand, have themselves agreed with them to be their servants, after they have been in the house a while think their master’s house a prison to them, muttering and grumbling against being kept in so strictly, as they regard it. And when they are called in to any religious exercise, they will be ready to say, “I was not hired for this, and I am not bound to you in this. Set me to your work and I will do it.”

3\. Some again are the more negligent and dissolute in their #footnote[3\. Negligent because their masters are religious.] business because their masters are religious. It is a common complaint that profane, worldly masters get more service than religious and godly masters, even though #emph[the godly] give better wages, food and lodging than #emph[the worldly.] Many think the fault for this lies in the master; but assuredly, for the most part, the fault is the corrupt disposition of servants, who value pleasure in sin, and freedom for it, more than wages, food and lodging. By this they show themselves ungodly toward God, ungrateful to their masters and harmful to themselves. Let this perverse disposition be noted, so that the apostle’s rule, very pertinent to this point, may be better observed: “Those #footnote[1 Tim 6:2.] who have believing masters should not show them disrespect, but should serve them all the more, since they are beloved believers.”

=== §. 17. #emph[Of servants’ obedience to reproof and correction.]

The same directions may serve for yielding to reproof and correction, for both may be brought under one head, and in general they agree. For reproof is a correction in words, and correction is a reproof in deed.

The obedience which servants must show their masters with respect to these is twofold:

1\. To bear patiently every kind of reproof and correction.

2\. To amend readily what they are justly reproved or corrected for.

For the first of these, servants have an express precept, enforced #footnote[1 Pet 2:18. #emph[etc.]] by many reasons, in these words: “Servants, submit yourselves#super[+] to your#super[+] masters with all fear, not only to those who are good and gentle, but also to the perverse. For this is thankworthy, if anyone, for conscience toward God, endures grief, suffering wrongfully. How is it to your#super[+] credit if you#super[+] are buffeted for your#super[+] faults and you#super[+] take it patiently? But if you#super[+] do well and suffer for it, and you#super[+] take it patiently, this is acceptable before God.” Here we see:

1\. That correction is to be borne patiently by servants: if correction, then much more reproof.

2\. That though correction is unjustly inflicted, yet it is to be endured patiently; therefore much more when it is deserved.

3\. That #emph[buffeting,] and that from a perverse master, is to be borne; therefore much more the lighter correction of a good and gentle master. Joseph patiently endured #emph[fetters,]#footnote[Ps 105:18.] and #emph[iron chains,] and #emph[imprisonment]#footnote[Gen 39:20.] inflicted on him most unjustly, even for his faithfulness to his master. Great was the recompense God made him; and assuredly God will recompense all the wrong, pain and grief that is endured for conscience’ sake in this case. For the apostle says that this is #emph[thankworthy,] that is, worthy of praise and commendation, and so worthy of recompense and reward. Again he says that it is #emph[acceptable to God,] so that God will recompense it, though men may count it nothing but servility and dullness; indeed, though unjust masters may take occasion from it to trample all the more on their servants and lord it over them.

To these motives I may add the rule of the Christian faith we profess (which the apostle also hints at in these words, #footnote[1 Pet 2:21.] “For to this you#super[+] were called”). For our Christian calling requires all men to “give place to wrath”;#footnote[Rom 12:19.] #footnote[Matt 5:39.] to #emph[turn the other cheek when one is struck;] #footnote[Rom 12:21.] to “overcome evil with good.” #footnote[1 Pet 2:21.] If all Christians must show such patience to all men, even their equals, how much more must servants show it to masters? The apostle commends to servants the example of Christ in this case: #emph[he was reviled,] and much #emph[he suffered,] and that most unjustly; and yet he bore all patiently. If servants suffer with him in this way, they also will reign with him.

=== §. 18. #emph[Of the extremes contrary to servants’ patient bearing of reproof and correction.]

Patient bearing of rebuke and blows seems #emph[a hard saying] to most servants: they cannot bear to hear it, for their mind and conduct are quite contrary to it.

1\. Some, when only reproved, though justly, are ready to answer back and to trade word for word: a sin #footnote[Titus 2:9 μὴ ἀντιλέγοντας.] expressly forbidden. It seems, from the apostle’s express mention of it, that #emph[answering back] has long been an evil trait in servants.

#emph[Objection.] If a master unjustly reproves his servant and the servant does not answer back, he knowingly allows his master to continue in his error, and so makes himself an accessory to his master’s sin.

#emph[Answer.] There is a difference between a spiteful, revengeful contradicting of what is said, and a humble, mild, reverent, timely defense of what is unjustly censured. #emph[The latter] is lawful, but #emph[the former] is forbidden.

2\. Others scorn to be corrected, and they show this disdain in many ways, such as:

1\. By muttering and saying #emph[they did not come for that.] But though that was not the main end of their subjection, yet it is a means to keep them in subjection, and is therefore to be endured by them.

2\. By running away, like #footnote[See §. 12.] Hagar.

3\. By struggling and striving with their master or mistress, and grabbing the stick or rod by the end, or by holding the hands of those who correct them.

#emph[Objection.] Must I let myself be wrongfully beaten when I can help myself and prevent it?

#emph[Answer.] 1. Servants must not be their own judges of whether their correction is just or unjust; for men are so prone to flatter themselves and to make light of the evil things they do that, if they were not corrected until they thought it just, they would never be corrected.

2\. To endure punishment (if I may use Saint Peter’s word) is #emph[thankworthy] in no other case. If you are justly punished, you have only what you deserve. If you are punished by force, so that you cannot resist, necessity makes you bear it. The sturdiest thieves there are, once their arms are bound, let themselves be hanged, because they see there is no help for it. But Christ (whose #footnote[1 Pet 2:21.] example in this case is set before servants) #footnote[Matt 26:53.] could have freed himself, but would not. If servants endure for conscience’ sake, they will not resist, though they are able.

3\. Others, if they are struck by master or mistress, will give as much as they receive: they will strike back. It is a practice unbecoming in any Christian, but most unseemly in Christian servants, who show by it contempt for God’s image and power in their masters.

4\. Others are so possessed by a devil that they will seek all the revenge they can if they are corrected. So it comes about that some hot, headstrong, reckless youths do not hesitate to challenge their masters to a duel, and others, more maliciously minded, secretly try to take their masters’ lives. Many who have not the opportunity to carry out such villainies nevertheless wish their masters’ destruction in their hearts and utter the most fearful curses against them; by this they make themselves guilty of blood before God.

=== §. 19. #emph[Of servants’ amending what they are justly reproved or corrected for.]

More than patience is required of those who are deservedly rebuked or corrected for their faults, namely #footnote[See more on this point Treatise 5, §. 33. #emph[&] 34.] repentance and amendment. In this way the smart and pain which servants endure will be like good medicine to them, and turn to their good. True amendment of former faults may make one a better servant than he was before he committed those faults. Witness what Saint Paul says of Onesimus: “Formerly he was useless to you, but now he has become useful both to you and to me.”#footnote[#emph[Philem. v.] 11.]

Contrary to this is the disposition of those who, despite all rebuke and correction, keep on in their evil and wicked courses, and go on provoking their master more and more, and so make their masters add blow to blow and stroke to stroke, until they have no hope of them and are forced to put them out of doors. This comes either from a scornful, disdainful pride (for “a scorner does not listen to rebuke”)#footnote[Prov 13:1.] or from a base, servile, numb, blockish, brutish nature that is not moved by any smart or pain, like a balky nag that will not stir however much it is whipped or beaten. Solomon#footnote[Prov 26:3.] implies as much by putting a horse, a donkey and a fool together in one leash, meaning by a fool a scornful, blockish servant, to whose back a rod is what a whip is to a horse. Of such a fool he says that #emph[a hundred lashes]#footnote[Prov 17:10.] do not sink into him; and again, “Though you grind a fool in a mortar with a pestle along with grain, yet his folly will not depart from him.”#footnote[Prov 27:22.]

But what shall we say of those who are made worse by rebuke and correction? What else, but that shame, beggary and some disgraceful death or other is likely to befall them?

So much for the kinds of servants’ duties. The next point concerns the manner of performing them.
